Here are the 2 easiest manual ways to animate a glowing light traveling through the maze from START to END in Blender (no coding).

First: Create Your "Light + Glowing Orb" at START

In Blender's Viewport Shading, a Point Light illuminates the stone walls around it, while a small Emissive Sphere makes the light itself visible as a glowing orb with Bloom glare. Grouping them takes 15 seconds:

Add the Point Light: Press Shift + A → Light → Point.
In Object Data Properties (green lightbulb icon on the right), pick a bright color and set Power to 200 W.
Add the Glowing Orb: Press Shift + A → Mesh → UV Sphere, then press S, type 0.22, and press Enter.
In Material Properties (red sphere icon), click the material dropdown icon (next to + New) and pick GlareMesh_Cyan, GlareMesh_StartGreen, or GlareMesh_EndGold so the ball glows with glare.
Link Them Together:
Click the Sphere, hold Shift and click the Point Light, then press Ctrl + P → Object (Keep Transform).
Now you only need to animate the Point Light, and the glowing orb will follow it automatically!
Method 1: The Smooth Pro Way (Curve + Follow Path Constraint)

Best if you want the light to glide smoothly along a drawn line from START to END:

Look from Top View: Press Numpad 7 (or click the blue +Z circle at the top-right of the 3D Viewport) so you can see the whole maze layout from above.
Draw the Maze Route:
Press Shift + A → Curve → Bezier, then press Tab to enter Edit Mode.
Press A then X → Vertices to delete the default 2 points.
Click the Draw tool on the left toolbar (the pencil/squiggle icon—press T if the toolbar is hidden).
Simply click and drag your mouse starting at START, tracing through the open corridors around the pits all the way to END.
Press Tab to go back to Object Mode, and move the curve slightly up (G → Z → 0.8 → Enter) so it floats inside the corridors above the floor.
Snap the Light onto the Curve:
Select your Point Light and press Alt + G (this resets its position to (0, 0, 0) so it snaps cleanly onto the curve without flying off to the side).
Go to Object Constraints Properties (the blue gear-with-chain icon on the right panel).
Click Add Object Constraint → Follow Path.
In Target, select your BezierCurve.
Keyframe START to END:
Check the Fixed Position box inside the Follow Path constraint.
At Frame 1 in the Timeline: set Offset Factor to 0.000, hover over it, and press I.
Move the Timeline to Frame 200 (or 250): set Offset Factor to 1.000, hover over it, and press I.
(Tip: If it moves backward from END to START, select the Curve → press Tab → press A → click Segments → Switch Direction at the top menu.)
Press Spacebar to watch the glowing orb light up the walls as it solves the maze!
Method 2: The Fast Beginner Way (Auto Keying Corner-by-Corner)

Because a maze is made of straight corridors, you can also just drag the light turn-by-turn without using curves at all:

Select your Point Light and move it (G) to the START gate at Z = 0.8.
In the Timeline at Frame 1, press I → Location.
Turn ON the round Record button (Auto Keying) at the bottom-center of the Timeline (next to the Play buttons).
Switch to Top View (Numpad 7):
Move the Timeline blue playhead to Frame 25, press G, and drag the light to the first corner of the corridor. (Blender automatically records the keyframe!)
Move the playhead to Frame 50, press G, and drag it to the next corner.
Repeat every 20–25 frames through the corridors until you reach END around Frame 200.
Turn OFF the round Record button (Auto Keying) when finished so you don't accidentally record extra moves, then press Spacebar to play!
